is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
OSPF control-plane and the core interlinks are stable. On all three core routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) the OSPF adjacencies are Full with hello/dead timers aligned (10s/40s). The “6 state changes” shown per neighbor are the normal transitions from Down→Full during initial convergence; importantly, the “Most recent state change” timestamps are several minutes in the past and there have been no subsequent resets. FRR logs corroborate this: each router only logged the initial Database Description negotiation at ~06:39:05 and no neighbor down/up events afterward.

Links between the core and to the server farm are clean: ethtool shows link detected and 10G/full; ip -s link and tc fq_codel counters show zero errors/drops across eth0/eth1 on all core routers and eth2 on router_core_3. OSPF route tables are consistent (core_1/core_2 learn 10.200.0.0/24 via router_core_3), and topology-wide reachability to the server farm is 100% with low RTT.

Localization: core triangle (router_core_1 eth0/eth1, router_core_2 eth0/eth1, router_core_3 eth0/eth1) and router_core_3–switch_server_access (eth2–eth5) all stable. There is no evidence of a flapping device or backbone link at this time. Root cause: the observed “flapping” appears to have been initial OSPF convergence after boot or a monitoring false alarm.

Corrective fix: No restart is needed. If alarms persist, enable brief OSPF adjacency logging (debug ospf adj) and consider setting “ip ospf network point-to-point” on /31 core links to avoid DR/BDR election noise, and/or deploy BFD for faster, definitive flap detection. 
=== END ===